//
// THE LOOP. A Loop is driven by one thread: that thread submits to it and
// polls it. It is a resource: when it dies it cancels every operation still
// in flight, closes every handle it holds, and waits for the kernel to give
// each buffer back before it frees anything (I18). Answers it owed to its own
// thread are dropped then, their buffers going home to their pools; answers
// owed to another loop or thread are still delivered there. A job running
// on the pool (or an external operation's Completer not yet ended) is not
// waited for: the old API's is answered Cancelled at the death, without its
// buffer, which the job still holds; the record stays the job's, and when its
// thread finds the loop gone it dissolves it there, the buffer going home (THE
// BLOCKING POOL). The loop lets go of its records at the end of its death, in
// one step under its port's lock with any job's post home, then takes what was
// posted before: so no job's record lands in the port after that, and a job
// whose answer goes back to its own loop (Target.Post) cannot hold the port
// alive from inside it. A job's post that Windows refuses while its loop
// still lives (no room for the packet) is tried again every millisecond,
// since that loop still holds the record. Loops on other threads are other
// Loops: one per driving thread. One exception to "one thread": a receive or
// a send may be ISSUED on another thread through the loop's shared table and
// handed to it in flight (WORKER-SIDE ISSUE).
//
// OPERATIONS. Each data-carrying operation takes an OWNER of its bytes, never
// a borrow: an IoBuf moved in, which the kernel writes into (Recv, ReadAt, at
// the end of what it holds, into its room) or reads from (Send, WriteAt, all
// it holds), and which the answer hands back; or Bytes, shared and read-only,
// for a send fanned out to many sockets. The caller no longer holds the
// buffer while the kernel does, so nothing can touch it, and a borrowed
// slice cannot be submitted at all: an Op's fields are IoBuf and Bytes, and a
// '&Array[u8]' or '&mut IoBuf' is neither (test/cases/concurrency/
// concurrency_iocore_submit.cone pins the refusals).
//
// - Connect(addr): a new TCP socket connected to addr; its Handle comes back
//   in the answer ('c.handle'). Accept(listener): the next connection on a
//   listening socket, likewise. Every socket iocore makes has Nagle's
//   algorithm off (TCP_NODELAY), as Go's do: a small send goes at once.
//   TCP keepalive is the asker's to set, on a socket once connected
//   ('setKeepAlive', a KeepAlive: httpconn sets its Config's).
// - Recv(sock, buf): up to buf's room; Done(n), the n bytes added to its
//   length; Done(0) is the end of the stream. A buffer with no room is
//   refused (Failed, ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER; Linux's EINVAL: the kind
//   InvalidInput), since a zero-byte receive
//   means something else:
// - RecvReady(sock): a zero-byte receive, holding no buffer at all: Done(0)
//   once data has arrived (or the stream has ended), for an idle connection,
//   so that thousands of them pin no memory. Then a Recv takes what came.
// - Send(sock, buf), SendBytes(sock, bytes): all of it; Done(n), n the bytes
//   sent.
// - SendChain(sock, bufs): every unconsumed byte of an iobuf Chain, its
//   segments in order, in ONE operation (a gather write: one kernel call
//   where the socket takes it all, not one per buffer); Done(n), n the
//   bytes sent. The answer gives the whole chain back ('c.bufs', or
//   'c.chainBack()'), every segment in it, however it ended. Windows hands
//   the segments to one WSASend as an array of WSABUFs (Winsock captures the
//   array at the call, so it is built on the stack, or for more than 16
//   segments in a block freed at once); Linux sends them with sendmsg over an
//   iovec of at most 64 segments a call, and a send the socket takes only in
//   part goes on from the byte it reached, across segment boundaries, as
//   Send's does. An empty chain is Done(0). The record holds the chain out of
//   line, 'bufs', an Option of an owning pointer (8 bytes, None for every
//   other operation), so no other operation's record is larger for it.
// - ReadAt(file, at, buf), WriteAt(file, at, buf): at the byte offset 'at',
//   with no cursor shared between operations; reading past the end is
//   Done(0).
//
// CHILDREN. 'lp.adoptChild(raw)' gives the loop a child process, a Handle of
// its own kind, for WaitChild. What 'raw' is, and what the loop owns, differ by
// system. Windows: raw is the process HANDLE (SYNCHRONIZE and
// PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION), and the loop OWNS it from the call,
// whatever the result; 'close' closes it. Linux: raw is the process id, and the
// loop owns only the pidfd it opens for it (pidfd_open, Linux 5.3 and later);
// the pid stays the caller's. WaitChild(child) answers when the process has
// ended, with no thread waiting for it: Done(n), n the exit code, and on Linux,
// for a process a signal ended, n = 0 and 'c.code' the signal's number (code is
// 0 in every other Done). The child is waited for once, on both systems: the
// WaitChild that answers Done is the only one that does, and a second on the
// same handle fails (Linux: ECHILD, the first reaped the child; Windows: the
// handle keeps a 'waited' flag, and the failure is ERROR_WAIT_NO_CHILDREN, 128).
// A cancelled or timed out wait has answered for nothing. Linux: a child never
// waited for stays a zombie until its parent reaps it. A WaitChild can be cancelled,
// can have a deadline, and is cancelled by closing the handle, like any other
// operation.
//
// PIPES. 'lp.adoptPipe(raw)' gives the loop one end of a pipe -- on Windows a
// HANDLE opened FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED (a named pipe's end), on Linux a
// descriptor, made non-blocking -- as a Handle of its own kind, owned by the
// loop from then on ('close' closes it; a failed adopt has). From the first
// adopted pipe, SIGPIPE is ignored process-wide; a write to a closed reader
// fails with an error instead (EPIPE; Windows has no such signal: its error
// is ERROR_NO_DATA or ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE).
// - PipeRead(pipe, buf): what the pipe holds, at most buf's room, added to
//   its length; Done(n). Done(0) is the end of the stream: the other end is
//   closed and everything it wrote is read. A buffer with no room is refused,
//   as Recv's is. The pipe's own capacity is the back-pressure: a writer
//   blocks (or its write waits, here) while nobody reads.
// - PipeWrite(pipe, buf): all of buf's bytes, across as many writes as the
//   pipe takes, Done(len); the answer comes when the last is taken.
// A pipe operation can be cancelled, can have a deadline and belongs to a
// cancel group like any other; a cancelled read has taken nothing.
//
// DATAGRAMS. 'lp.bindUdp(addr)' is a UDP socket bound to addr (port 0 has the
// OS choose one; 'localAddr' tells it), a Handle of its own kind: the stream
// operations fail on it as on a handle that names nothing (ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE,
// EBADF: InvalidInput), as the datagram ones do on a TCP socket. It is not connected; each datagram names its other end.
// - RecvFrom(sock, buf): ONE datagram, into buf's room (a buffer with no room
//   is refused, as Recv's is); Done(n), the n bytes added to its length, and
//   the sender in the answer's 'c.peer', a SocketAddr. A datagram of no bytes
//   is Done(0): unlike a stream's, it is not an end. A datagram longer than
//   the room is cut: Failed with the system's number for it (WSAEMSGSIZE,
//   10040; Linux's EMSGSIZE, 90), which 'c.error()' reads as the kind
//   Truncated, the room filled with its first bytes (the length says so), the
//   sender named, the rest of that datagram lost; the next one is whole. An
//   operation that took no datagram names no sender (an address of size 0, its
//   port 0).
// - SendTo(sock, addr, buf): all of buf as ONE datagram to addr; Done(n), n its
//   bytes; 'c.peer' is addr. A datagram too large for the network is Failed
//   with the same number, of the kind Other (nothing was cut on receipt). UDP promises nothing about delivery: Done says the
//   system took the datagram.
// - Deadlines, cancel groups, Requests and handle close work as for TCP, the
//   answer carrying the buffer back; a receive no datagram has met is the
//   operation that waits (Linux: in the socket's queue; Windows: in the
//   kernel). One receive at a time per socket is the supported use: the order
//   in which several outstanding receives are filled is not promised. The
//   datagrams that arrive while none is outstanding wait in the socket's
//   buffer, which the OS bounds (further ones are lost, as UDP's are).
// - Windows' habit of failing a socket's next receive when an earlier datagram
//   it sent met a closed port (the ICMP port-unreachable, SIO_UDP_CONNRESET) is
//   turned off, so the sender's receive just goes on waiting, as Linux's does.
// 'c.peer' is also Connect's address in a Connect's answer; for every other
// operation it is of size 0. Batching (recvmmsg, sendmmsg) is not here yet.
//
// TARGETS. Where an operation's one answer goes. Target.Here(token): to this
// loop's own 'poll'. Target.Post(token, waker): to ANOTHER loop, the one the
// waker wakes, posted into its port, so that a thread idle in its own poll
// wakes the instant the answer exists -- how a frame loop learns of a read a
// service thread finished. The token is plain data the loop hands back and
// never reads (I11): the driver decides what it means. Three targets hand
// the answer to another thread with no loop of its own:
//
// - Target.Chan(token, tx): sent on a sync channel (a Sender[Completion]);
//   the receiving thread recv's it. The loop never waits on the channel: a
//   bounded one that is full has the answer held by the loop and tried
//   again every pass of poll (poll waits at most a millisecond while any is
//   held), so it is never lost or sent twice; a channel whose receiver has
//   gone drops the answer, its buffer going home (counted).
// - Target.Once(token, tx): sent on a sync one-shot (a OneshotSender) a
//   thread waits on: a synchronous call, the caller blocking on its
//   OneshotReceiver while the loop's thread does the operation.
// - Target.Status(token, board, slot): left in slot 'slot' of a StatusArray,
//   which a thread looks at when it chooses and never waits on (DirectStorage's
//   status arrays): 'isReady(i)', then 'take(i, out)'. The slot is claimed
//   at submit and free again once taken; a slot given a second operation
//   while it holds one, or out of range, is a panic at the caller's line.
//
// The last hands the answer to another runtime, which the loop knows nothing
// of (I11): Target.Hook(token, sink), where a Sink is a closure of that
// runtime's own, a Uni[fn(c Completion) + Sendable] holding what it needs. The
// loop calls it with the answer on its own thread, once, where it would have
// sent the answer; a Sink that dies without delivering (its loop gone before
// the operation was started, say) finalizes the closure instead, and what the
// closure holds is finalized with it. The closure runs inside poll, or inside
// the loop's death, so it hands the answer on and never calls back into the
// loop. No package of the runtime uses it now: the actor runtime's I/O thread
// (the aio package) keeps records of its own, starts them itself and takes
// them back answered (RECORDS), with no Completion and no closure between.
//
// Each of these answers is stamped delivered when the loop hands it over,
// and a loop whose answers all go elsewhere still sees its poll return as
// each goes.
//
// ANSWERS. Every operation gets exactly one terminal answer, a Completion
// (I1): its OpId, its token, its kind (diag's kind numbers), its outcome --
// Done with a count, Failed with the system's own error number (Windows'
// on Windows, the errno on Linux; 'c.error()' is it as an IoError, ERRORS),
// Cancelled, or TimedOut -- the buffer back ('c.buf', IoBuf() when it
// had none; a SendChain's chain in 'c.bufs', None for every other operation), the
// handle Connect or Accept made, and three instants: submitted, dequeued
// (when the loop took it from the kernel's queue: IOCP does not say when the
// kernel finished, so this is the earliest the loop can know; on Linux, when
// the loop tried the operation and it was done or failed) and delivered
// (when poll handed it over, or the loop it was posted to took it). The
// outcome is in plain fields ('c.status', a diag outcome number, 'c.n',
// 'c.code'), and 'c.outcome()' gives it as the Outcome enum, to match on. A
// buffer comes out of a Completion the code owns by a field list, 'let buf =
// c.(buf)' (the rest of the answer goes at the end of the statement), or
// 'let h, buf = c.(handle, buf)' for more than one; one left in dies with
// it, home to its pool. A Completion is Sendable: it may go to another thread.
//
// ERRORS. An IoError says what went wrong in two ways. Its kind, an
// IoErrorKind of the ioerror package (a leaf the fs package shares), is the
// same on every system: NotFound, PermissionDenied, ConnectionRefused,
// ConnectionReset, AddressInUse, TimedOut, ... (Rust's io::ErrorKind, trimmed
// to what the two systems give here), and Other for a number no kind names;
// a caller matches on it ('e.kind() == IoErrorKind.ConnectionRefused', with
// 'import ioerror use IoErrorKind'). Its code is the system's own number, kept
// for diagnostics and the message: Windows' error number on Windows, the
// errno on Linux, so the same failure has a different number on each (a
// refused connection: 1225 and 111). ioerror sorts each system's numbers into
// kinds by a table of its own (osKind). Cancelled and TimedOut from a cancel
// or a deadline carry code 0 (the system aborting an operation,
// ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED or ECANCELED, is Cancelled too, with its number);
// Truncated, a datagram cut to the room, carries the system's number for a
// datagram too long. 'c.error()' (or 'answerError') turns an answer that is
// not Done into its IoError; 'IoError(code)' is the system's error of a
// number got elsewhere (the process package's, say). IoError stays here, not
// in ioerror, because its words are the loop's system calls' (FormatMessageW,
// strerror, gai_strerror).
//
// CANCEL. 'lp.cancel(id)' asks an operation to end. It is a request: the
// operation still answers exactly once, and its answer says how it ended --
// Cancelled if the cancel caught it, or Done (or Failed) if it had finished
// first; the buffer comes back either way. It is idempotent: true while the
// operation's answer is still to come (asking twice does nothing more), false
// once its answer is decided or for an id that names nothing. A timer
// cancelled answers Cancelled, decided there and then (so a second cancel
// of it says false). Closing a handle cancels everything in flight
// on it; 'lp.cancelHandle(h, write)' asks every operation in flight on h in
// one direction to end, the old API's and an asker's records alike: its reads
// (accept, receive, RecvFrom, ReadAt) or its writes (send, SendTo, WriteAt,
// Linux's connect; Windows' connect has no handle until it has connected),
// each answering as a cancel's does, and says how many it asked (one asked
// already is not asked again). A job queued on the blocking pool is taken out of its queue and
// answered Cancelled at once, its buffer back; one running cannot be
// stopped, and answers how it ended when it returns (one answered early, by a
// deadline, leaves its record to the job until then). An external operation's
// Completer is told (EXTERNAL OPERATIONS). Cancel does not abandon anything:
// what is still the kernel's stays in its record until it is not.
//
// DEADLINES. A deadline is an absolute Instant, never a duration (I6):
// 'submitBy', or the deadline of 'submitIn', whichever is tighter with the
// group's. An operation whose deadline has already passed is answered
// TimedOut at submit and never started. At the deadline the loop asks the
// operation's cancel, and the answer says TimedOut. Then the kernel (or the
// pool's thread) has the loop's grace -- 1 ms unless 'setGrace' says
// otherwise -- to give the buffer back: a socket's or a local file's cancel
// is answered in microseconds, and the TimedOut comes with its buffer. Past
// the grace the loop answers TimedOut at once WITHOUT the buffer, promptly,
// as a frame loop needs even when a read on a hung share is stuck; the
// buffer goes home to its pool when the kernel or the job lets it go (a
// late return, counted), and the record waits for it (RECORDS: for an
// asker's record this is the one bad case). After such a prompt TimedOut the operation's effect is unknown: a
// receive may have taken bytes, a send may have sent. If the kernel answers
// between the loop's decision and its delivery, the kernel's own outcome is
// the answer, buffer and all. A job still queued at its deadline is taken
// out of its queue and answered TimedOut with its buffer, never run.
//
// CANCEL GROUPS. A CancelGroup is a value -- copied, handed to many
// operations, on any loops, passed between threads (Sendable) -- whose one
// 'cancel()' asks every operation in it to end: the browser's AbortSignal,
// tokio's cancellation token. Each operation still answers exactly once,
// saying how it ended. Cancelled is a state, not an event (I7): from the
// cancel on, 'isCancelled()' is true, and an operation submitted in the
// group is answered Cancelled at once, its buffer back, never started.
// 'cancel' is idempotent: true the first time. 'g.child()' is cancelled
// with g, and by its own cancel, which does not reach g; 'g.childUntil(d)'
// is a child whose deadline is the tighter of g's and d -- deadlines are
// inherited as the tighter, all the way down. 'CancelGroup.until(d)' is a
// group with a deadline: every operation in it answers TimedOut by d, and a
// timer (afterIn) due after d answers TimedOut at d, one due before it Done. A
// shield, 'g.shield(d)', is for cleanup after a cancel: g's cancel and
// deadline do not reach it, its own deadline d bounds it, and its own
// cancel still ends it. A group cancelled from another thread posts a
// packet to each loop with operations in it, so a loop asleep in poll wakes
// at once; the loop then asks the cancel of each of its operations in a
// cancelled group. The group itself is the cancelgroups package's (iocore
// re-exports it), which actors and 'parallel each' reach as well: its header
// says how it is built, and why 'isCancelled()' is a flag load per level of
// ancestry with no count touched. A group with a deadline of its own also
// arms a timer that cancels it with the cause TimedOut ('g.cause()' says
// why a group ended); the loop does not treat that as a cancel, and answers
// its operations TimedOut from their deadlines, as it always did.
//
// REQUESTS. 'lp.request(id)' is a Request: a handle on operation id that
// asks its cancel when it dies -- dropping it sends a cancel and abandons
// nothing; the operation still answers, to its target. It is Sendable: a
// thread that is not the loop's holds it, and its 'cancel()' (idempotent)
// or its death posts the cancel to the loop, which acts on it at its next
// poll, waking if it sleeps. 'detach()' lets it go without cancelling; a
// Request that dies after its operation's answer posts a cancel the loop
// finds names nothing, so a holder that knows the answer has come detaches.
//
// THE BLOCKING POOL. Work with no completion form -- a file's metadata, a
// name looked up (getaddrinfo), a file opened and read where no overlapped
// form will do -- runs on the pool's threads and is answered into the loop
// that asked, like the kernel's answers, under the same contract:
// Op.Call(lane, work, arg, buf), 'work' a plain function '&fn(arg u64, buf
// &mut IoBuf) Outcome' run on one of the lane's threads with the buffer
// moved to it, its Outcome the answer (Done with a count, or Failed). One
// pool, in lanes: each lane its own bounded queue and its own threads
// (between a least and a most, started as jobs wait and leaving after an
// idle time), so a slow resolver holds up only other look-ups, never the
// disk. Lane.general() (4 threads, 1,024 queued) and Lane.dns() (2 threads,
// 256 queued) every process has; 'addLane(LaneConfig(name: ...,
// minThreads: ..., maxThreads: ..., queueBound: ..., idleMillis: ...))' makes
// more (64 in all), one per storage device for the file service to come. A
// submit past a lane's bound is answered at once, Failed with ERROR_BUSY
// (Linux's EBUSY: ResourceBusy), its buffer back (I9). 'l.stats()' counts its queue depth now and at most,
// threads, jobs run, cancelled out of the queue and refused, and the time
// jobs waited queued. The pool comes with three kinds of work, each an Op
// built by a function: 'resolveOp(host, port, buf)' (DNS lane; Done(n), the
// buffer holding n addresses for 'addressesIn'), 'statOp(path, lane, buf)'
// (Done; 'fileStatIn(&buf)' gives size, attributes and modified time) and
// 'readFileOp(path, at, lane, buf)' (Done(n), the bytes from 'at'); each path
// is a '&Path' (a string literal where one is wanted is made one), as is
// 'lp.openFile''s. The
// lanes and their threads live as long as the process.
//
// A job is its RECORD (RECORDS), and nothing is allocated for it: the record is
// queued on its lane, and the lane's thread is handed its address, runs the
// work on the record's own buffer where it lies, writes the outcome into the
// record and posts its address home, as the kernel does an operation's. The
// rule while it is AWAY, a promise the package keeps on trust (no type says
// it): from the moment a record is queued on a lane until it is posted home,
// the pool's thread owns the record's job part (its outcome, its waker, Linux's
// descriptor) and its buffer, and reads its request ('work', 'at', 'lane',
// 'kind'), which nobody changes then; the loop touches only the record's
// cancel flag (to ask a cancel) and, under the lane's lock, its queue links and
// job state (to take a job still queued back). An external operation's record
// is away the same way from its start, its Completer the owner. The rest of the
// record (its promise, its lists, its timer) stays the loop's. A record away
// keeps its POOL alive: a pool dies when its owner has let it go and its last
// record away is home (one count, the pool's holds), so a loop that dies with
// a job running lets its record go, the job's thread finding the loop gone
// dissolves it where it is, its answer abandoned and its buffer home, and the
// last one home frees the pool.
//
// EXTERNAL OPERATIONS. An operation whose answer comes from outside the
// kernel and the pool -- another thread, a GPU fence's watcher, a library
// with callbacks of its own -- is made by 'lp.external(to, buf)' (or
// 'externalIn', with a group and a deadline), which answers a Completer.
