// answer is delivered into the loop, to the operation's target, waking a
// poll that sleeps. Everything else holds as for any operation: its cancel
// (by lp.cancel, a Request, its group) is told to the Completer
// ('isCancelRequested()'), which gives the answer; its deadline gives a
// prompt TimedOut past the grace, and a later ending is dropped, the buffer
// going home; a Completer that dies without an ending ends its operation
// Failed with ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED (Linux's ECANCELED: 'IoError.aborted()',
// of the kind Cancelled), so it still answers exactly once. The
// Completer holds the operation's record while it is away (THE BLOCKING POOL):
// one that outlives its loop (answered Cancelled at the death, its cancel
// asked) still writes into 'buf()', and its ending, false then, dissolves the
// record on its own thread.
//
// RECORDS. Every operation is ONE RECORD, which the loop owns while it works on
// it: an OpRec[A], the loop's OpCore first (the request, what the operation
// owns, the promise, the answer) and the asker's own A after it. The calls
// above take records from the loop's own pool, their A the target and the
// token, and answer each with a Completion. A layer with answers of its own
// (the aio package) keeps a pool of its own, an OpPool[A], and starts its
// records itself:
